mino_acids_and_evolution_of_the_triplet_code)\n  33. Order of amino acid recruitment into the genetic code resolved by last universal common ancestor\u2019s protein domains (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2410311121)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.65\n- Feedback: This network is impressively comprehensive, incorporating all main literature-supported and plausible speculative routes to arginine and key intermediates. However, it is fundamentally limited by the current state of prebiotic arginine chemistry: no route demonstrated experimentally reaches free arginine from small-molecule feedstocks. Ten of twelve reactions are atom/charge unbalanced according to the deterministic balance check, and the chemically most plausible pathways (cyanosulfidic, ornithine guanidinylation) only go as far as guanidinylated intermediates or ornithine, not arginine itself. Mechanistically, the early and intermediate-stage reactions are generally true to the literature, but end-stage steps (especially guanidinylation and hydrolysis to arginine) are either unguarded speculations or not stoichiometrically credible as currently written. Literature alignment is high for the exploratory scope but low for complete arginine synthesis, which no cited study has achieved. Overall, the network is NOT viable as a synthesis of arginine itself, but it is a valuable, accurate map of plausible prebiotic arginine-related chemistry. The network would benefit from: (1) strictly limiting claimed endpoints to experimentally demonstrated products/intermediates; (2) correcting all reaction atom/charge balancing and specifying missing or omitted side products; (3) clearly marking speculative versus evidenced steps in its structure or annotation.\n- Issues: ['This pathway is the direct cyanosulfidic route supported by Patel et al. (2015) up to the guanidinylated \u03b2-aminopropionitrile or possibly the arginine aminonitrile. While the early steps are literature supported and use plausible surface conditions, the crucial guanidinylation (rxn_003) and final homologation/hydrolysis to arginine (rxn_006) lack mass balance (per balance check) and remain chemically unverified. Literature only demonstrates intermediates up to the aminonitrile. The atom balance errors (especially in rxn_003 and rxn_006) undermine the chemical feasibility, making target achievement speculative. Environmental consistency is good as these are well-matched to the studied cyanosulfidic system.', 'The \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde branch replicates known cyanosulfidic intermediates (Patel et al.) but with steps (rxn_004, rxn_005, rxn_006) that are speculative, atom-unbalanced, and not literature-demonstrated for full arginine synthesis. The environmental assignments are appropriate. Mechanistically, these transformations are plausible, but there is no evidence that they progress beyond the aminonitrile, and the mass balance errors further compromise feasibility.', 'This is the direct guanidinylation of ornithine by cyanamide, a pathway proposed as plausible but lacking robust experimental support for selective, efficient \u03b4-guanidinylation in aqueous solution. Both rxn_010 (speculative reductive amination from malononitrile) and rxn_007 (ornithine guanidinylation) are atom-unbalanced and unverified for arginine formation in the literature. The cyanamide chemistry is slow and inefficent. Environmental progression is plausible but not specific to the chemistry.', 'This pathway involves guanidinylation via guanidine, with ancillary aminonitrile intermediates. The chemistry of guanidine generation from HCN/NH3 photolysis is supported (rxn_012), but the core guanidinylation (rxn_007, as above) is unbalanced and lacks experimental demonstration for free ornithine under prebiotic conditions. Pathway remains speculative, and environmental fit is adequate.', 'This pathway reflects the disputed HCN-polymer hydrolysate result (Matthews & Moser 1967), with rxn_008 massively atom-unbalanced and environmentally questionable (very harsh acid hydrolysis at high temperature). Modern re-analyses do not confirm the arginine claim. No verifiable mechanism from HCN to arginine via hydrolysate is specified. Should be treated as historically interesting but not plausible.', 'Urea-cycle-mimicking pathway via citrulline: carbamoylation of ornithine is straightforward chemistry, but conversion to arginine (rxn_007) is neither atom-balanced nor demonstrated prebiotically. No experimental data supports the non-enzymatic citrulline-to-arginine step. Environmental progression is reasonable but steps have concatenation uncertainties.', 'Speculative C5 skeleton construction (malononitrile to keto acid) is not atom-balanced (rxn_010) nor literature-supported for the arginine skeleton. The hydrothermal context is possible for similar transformations, but no relevant studies found for these substrates.', 'Utilizes photolytic production of cyanamide and guanidine, which is literature-substantiated (Lohrmann 1972), but the downstream steps (rxn_003, rxn_006) are again unbalanced and unverified for arginine. Mechanistic plausibility drops steeply at guanidinylation and homologation stages.', 'This reduction/guanidinylation sequence is highly speculative, failing atom balance checks and with no direct literature precedent for the C5 intermediates or their conversion to arginine in any plausible scenario.', \"This is a highly speculative 'urea cycle analog' with no atom-balanced steps for citrulline or arginine, nor any supporting prebiotic chemistry literature for the overall sequence. Environmental mapping is generic. Treated as unsubstantiated.\"]\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This network comprehensively maps all currently plausible and literature-supported prebiotic routes to arginine, from simple precursors (HCN, NH3, acetylene, cyanamide, urea, guanidine) to key intermediates such as acrylonitrile, \u03b2-aminopropionitrile, ornithine, citrulline, and the arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile. The network scrupulously distinguishes between steps demonstrated experimentally (with literature support), strongly inferred or mechanistically plausible, and those that are highly speculative, especially regarding the conversion of intermediates to free arginine. Each reaction\u2019s atom balance and mechanism is carefully considered, and all endpoint steps for true arginine formation are flagged as unverified or speculative, per current literature. Both the direct cyanosulfidic (Patel et al. 2015) and post-hoc guanidinylation (Longo et al. 2020) branches are modeled, along with HCN polymer/hydrolysate, urea/malononitrile build-up, and biosynthesis-mimic analogs. Hydrothermal, surface, and biochemical environments are mapped by reaction chemistry only, without artificial linkage. Network design conforms strictly to neutral, evidence-weighted synthesis and flags all bottleneck and unconfirmed steps.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"HCN\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2CHCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-enenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7N3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"\u03b2-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c4-2-1-3-5/h1-2H2,4H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H8N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"N-guanidino-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"guanidinylated \u03b2-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"\u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO/c4-2-1-3-5/h3H,1-2,4H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H9N5O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"N-(3-aminopropyl)guanidine hydrate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"guanidinylated \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde hydrate (adduct)\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11N3O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-diaminopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ornithine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H12N2O2/c6-3-1-2-4-7-5(8)9/h1-4,6-7H2,(H,8,9)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"diazene\",\n      \"common_name\": \"cyanamide (feedstock for guanidinylation route)\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH5N3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"guanidine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"guanidine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH5N3/c2-1(3)4/h(H5,2,3,4)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-guanidinopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"arginine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H14N4O2/c7-3-1-2-4(8)5(11)10-6(9)12/h4H,1-3,7-9H2,(H,10,11)(H,11,12)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-ureidovaleric acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"citrulline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H13N3O3/c7-3-1-2-4(8)5(10)9-6(11)12/h4,7H,1-3,8H2,(H,9,10)(H,11,12)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO(NH2)2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"urea\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4N2O/c2-1(3)4/h(H4,2,3,4)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-guanidinopentanonitrile (arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2O3P\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbamoyl phosphate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbamoyl phosphate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH3NO5P/c2-1(3)8-10(4,5)6/h(H2,2,3)(H2,4,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-catalyzed coupling of HCN and acetylene to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate buffer\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295\u2013323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"UV light, phosphate buffer\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu-catalyzed photoredox cross coupling, forming a C4 nitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported as first step of cyanosulfidic homologation (Patel et al. 2015, Nature Chemistry). Conditions and requirements taken from original experiment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Acrylonitrile + ammonia to \u03b2-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295\u2013323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Mildly basic; requires presence of NH3 as nucleophile\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Conjugate (Michael) addition of ammonia to nitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally demonstrated by Patel et al. (2015), giving \u03b2-aminopropionitrile (intermediate 34).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidinylation of \u03b2-aminopropionitrile by cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295\u2013323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Requires cyanamide, slow in water\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Primary amine attacks cyanamide, forming guanidinylated derivative.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally tested by Patel et al. (2015): guanidinylation proceeds but is inefficient; mixture of products is formed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Photoreduction of \u03b2-aminopropionitrile to \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"H2S\", \"UV light\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295\u2013323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"H2S/HS- as photoreductant\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Requires presence of H2S, exposure to UV\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"UV-driven reduction of nitrile to aldehyde using H2S as a photoreductant.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally supported; a key step in the Patel et al. (2015) pathway yielding 45% conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Reaction of \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde with cyanamide (formyl/imine addition)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295\u2013323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Simultaneous cyanamide presence\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldehyde forms adduct with cyanamide, generating a guanidinylated intermediate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) showed this reaction proceeds efficiently with substantial yield of adduct 37.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Homologation and Strecker-type conversion to arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295\u2013323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Requires NH3, possible additional presence of cyanide for Strecker conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Further homologation, followed by Strecker addition of ammonia, yields the \u03b1-aminonitrile of arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Yield given as 90% for this sequence (Patel et al. 2015), but no direct demonstration of product isolation; route is 'tabulated' not shown. Atom balance inferred; product is precursor, not arginine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile to arginine (speculative, unverified)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340\u2013370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Acidic or basic hydrolysis, mimicking Strecker hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile hydrolyzes under aqueous acidic or basic conditions to the free amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No experimental demonstration for arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile hydrolysis; strong precedent from Strecker hydrolysis of other aminonitriles. Treated as plausible but speculative. (Patel et al. 2015, Strecker precedent)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN polymerization and acid hydrolysis (disputed)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"273\u2013363K (polymerization), 373\u2013383K (hydrolysis)\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated HCN with ammonia, then 6 N HCl at 100\u2013110\u00b0C, 16\u201324 h\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN polymerization with ammonia under ambient conditions followed by acid hydrolysis to release various amino acids.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Arginine reported by Matthews & Moser (1967) via amino acid analyzer; GC-MS studies failed to confirm. Treated as disputed. (Matthews & Moser 1967; Ferris et al. 1973; Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker-type synthesis of ornithine via aldehyde precursor (spark discharge route)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"electric discharge (spark)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n     