m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Arginine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: This network comprehensively maps all currently plausible and literature-supported prebiotic routes to arginine, from simple precursors (HCN, NH3, acetylene, cyanamide, urea, guanidine) to key intermediates such as acrylonitrile, \u03b2-aminopropionitrile, ornithine, citrulline, and the arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile. The network scrupulously distinguishes between steps demonstrated experimentally (with literature support), strongly inferred or mechanistically plausible, and those that are highly speculative, especially regarding the conversion of intermediates to free arginine. Each reaction\u2019s atom balance and mechanism is carefully considered, and all endpoint steps for true arginine formation are flagged as unverified or speculative, per current literature. Both the direct cyanosulfidic (Patel et al. 2015) and post-hoc guanidinylation (Longo et al. 2020) branches are modeled, along with HCN polymer/hydrolysate, urea/malononitrile build-up, and biosynthesis-mimic analogs. Hydrothermal, surface, and biochemical environments are mapped by reaction chemistry only, without artificial linkage. Network design conforms strictly to neutral, evidence-weighted synthesis and flags all bottleneck and unconfirmed steps.\n\n