.researchgate.net/publication/9309353_The_Hydrolysis_of_Arginine)\n32. [Reasons for the occurrence of the twenty coded protein ...](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=167961801406af19852f76f62e796fa905c99643&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\n33. [Reasons for the occurrence of the twenty coded protein amino acids - ADS](https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1981JMolE..17..273W)\n34. [Consensus temporal order of amino acids and evolution of the triplet code](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/12166018_Consensus_temporal_order_of_amino_acids_and_evolution_of_the_triplet_code)\n35. [Order of amino acid recruitment into the genetic code resolved by last universal common ancestor\u2019s protein domains](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2410311121)\n\n\nKey Citations:\n  1. Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism - Nature Chemistry (https://www.nature.com/articles/nchem.2202)\n  2. Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4568310/)\n  3. Chemical evolution (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01797455)\n  4. Chemical evolution: XXI. The Amino Acids Released on Hydrolysis of HCN Oligomers - ADS (https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1974JMolE...3..225F/abstract)\n  5. Possible role of hydrogen cyanide in chemical evolution: Investigation of the proposed direct synthesis of peptides from hydrogen cyanide - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0022283673900429)\n  6. (PDF) Primordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 Miller H 2 S-rich spark discharge experiment (https://www.academia.edu/91151237/Primordial_synthesis_of_amines_and_amino_acids_in_a_1958_Miller_H_2_S_rich_spark_discharge_experiment)\n  7. (PDF) The Miller Volcanic Spark Discharge Experiment (https://www.academia.edu/121732208/The_Miller_Volcanic_Spark_Discharge_Experiment)\n  8. Primordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 Miller H2S-rich spark discharge experiment (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1019191108)\n  9. Current status of the prebiotic synthesis of small molecules - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11542054/)\n  10. Primordial emergence of a nucleic acid-binding protein via phase separation and statistical ornithine-to-arginine conversion (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2001989117)\n  11. Primordial emergence of a nucleic acid-binding protein via phase separation and statistical ornithine-to-arginine conversion (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/342323056_Primordial_emergence_of_a_nucleic_acid-binding_protein_via_phase_separation_and_statistical_ornithine-to-arginine_conversion)\n  12. (PDF) Primordial emergence of a nucleic acid binding protein via phase separation and statistical ornithine to arginine conversion (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/338687343_Primordial_emergence_of_a_nucleic_acid_binding_protein_via_phase_separation_and_statistical_ornithine_to_arginine_conversion)\n  13. Chemical studies into the aetiology of proteinogenic amino acids Benjamin Thoma (https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10185022/2/B_Thoma_Thesis_Corrected.pdf)\n  14. Selective Synthesis of Lysine Peptides and the Prebiotically Plausible Synthesis of Catalytically Active Diaminopropionic Acid Peptide Nitriles in Water (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/jacs.2c12497)\n  15. The Urea Cycle Step by Step (https://www.news-medical.net/health/The-Urea-Cycle-Step-by-Step.aspx)\n  16. Sources and Fates of Carbamyl Phosphate: A Labile Energy-Rich Molecule with Multiple Facets (https://www.mdpi.com/2079-7737/7/2/34)\n  17. Prebiotic Synthesis of Aspartate Using Life\u2019s Metabolism as a Guide (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10221237/)\n  18. Quantifying Reductive Amination in Nonenzymatic Amino Acid Synthesis (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9828492/)\n  19. A plausible metal-free ancestral analogue of the Krebs cycle composed entirely of \u03b1-ketoacids (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41557-020-00560-7)\n  20. Formation of urea and guanidine by irradiation of ammonium cyanide (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01660246)\n  21. Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a (https://europepmc.org/api/getPdf?pmcid=PMC4568310)\n  22. Cyanosulfidic prebiotic synthesis (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyanosulfidic_prebiotic_synthesis)\n  23. Sulfidic Anion Concentrations on Early Earth for Surficial Origins-of-Life Chemistry (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1801.07725)\n  24. Guanidine synthesis by guanylation (https://www.organic-chemistry.org/synthesis/C1N/guanidines.shtm)\n  25. Extraterrestrial amino acids and L\u2010enantiomeric excesses in the \\<fc\\>CM\\</fc\\>2 carbonaceous chondrites Aguas Zarcas and Murchison - Glavin - 2021 - Meteoritics & Planetary Science - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/maps.13451)\n  26. The stereochemistry of amino acids in the Murchison meteorite - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0301926800001236)\n  27. Purines and triazines in the Murchison meteorite - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0016703775901015)\n  28. Extraterrestrial nucleobases in the Murchison meteorite - ScienceDirect (https://sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012821X08001866?via=ihub%3D)\n  29. (PDF) The Hydrolysis of Arginine (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/9309353_The_Hydrolysis_of_Arginine)\n  30. Reasons for the occurrence of the twenty coded protein ... (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=167961801406af19852f76f62e796fa905c99643&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\n  31. Reasons for the occurrence of the twenty coded protein amino acids - ADS (https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1981JMolE..17..273W)\n  32. Consensus temporal order of amino acids and evolution of the triplet code (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/12166018_Consensus_temporal_order_of_amino_acids_and_evolution_of_the_triplet_code)\n  33. Order of amino acid recruitment into the genetic code resolved by last universal common ancestor\u2019s protein domains (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2410311121)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.65\n- Feedback: This network is impressively comprehensive, incorporating all main literature-supported and plausible speculative routes to arginine and key intermediates. However, it is fundamentally limited by the current state of prebiotic arginine chemistry: no route demonstrated experimentally reaches free arginine from small-molecule feedstocks. Ten of twelve reactions are atom/charge unbalanced according to the deterministic balance check, and the chemically most plausible pathways (cyanosulfidic, ornithine guanidinylation) only go as far as guanidinylated intermediates or ornithine, not arginine itself. Mechanistically, the early and intermediate-stage reactions are generally true to the literature, but end-stage steps (especially guanidinylation and hydrolysis to arginine) are either unguarded speculations or not stoichiometrically credible as currently written. Literature alignment is high for the exploratory scope but low for complete arginine synthesis, which no cited study has achieved. Overall, the network is NOT viable as a synthesis of arginine itself, but it is a valuable, accurate map of plausible prebiotic arginine-related chemistry. The network would benefit from: (1) strictly limiting claimed endpoints to experimentally demonstrated products/intermediates; (2) correcting all reaction atom/charge balancing and specifying missing or omitted side products; (3) clearly marking speculative versus evidenced steps in its structure or annotation.\n- Issues: ['This pathway is the direct cyanosulfidic route supported by Patel et al. (2015) up to the guanidinylated \u03b2-aminopropionitrile or possibly the arginine aminonitrile. While the early steps are literature supported and use plausible surface conditions, the crucial guanidinylation (rxn_003) and final homologation/hydrolysis to arginine (rxn_006) lack mass balance (per balance check) and remain chemically unverified. Literature only demonstrates intermediates up to the aminonitrile. The atom balance errors (especially in rxn_003 and rxn_006) undermine the chemical feasibility, making target achievement speculative. Environmental consistency is good as these are well-matched to the studied cyanosulfidic system.', 'The \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde branch replicates known cyanosulfidic intermediates (Patel et al.) but with steps (rxn_004, rxn_005, rxn_006) that are speculative, atom-unbalanced, and not literature-demonstrated for full arginine synthesis. The environmental assignments are appropriate. Mechanistically, these transformations are plausible, but there is no evidence that they progress beyond the aminonitrile, and the mass balance errors further compromise feasibility.', 'This is the direct guanidinylation of ornithine by cyanamide, a pathway proposed as plausible but lacking robust experimental support for selective, efficient \u03b4-guanidinylation in aqueous solution. Both rxn_010 (speculative reductive amination from malononitrile) and rxn_007 (ornithine guanidinylation) are atom-unbalanced and unverified for arginine formation in the literature. The cyanamide chemistry is slow and inefficent. Environmental progression is plausible but not specific to the chemistry.', 'This pathway involves guanidinylation via guanidine, with ancillary aminonitrile intermediates. The chemistry of guanidine generation from HCN/NH3 photolysis is supported (rxn_012), but the core guanidinylation (rxn_007, as above) is unbalanced and lacks experimental demonstration for free ornithine under prebiotic conditions. Pathway remains speculative, and environmental fit is adequate.', 'This pathway reflects the disputed HCN-polymer hydrolysate result (Matthews & Moser 1967), with rxn_008 massively atom-unbalanced and environmentally questionable (very harsh acid hydrolysis at high temperature). Modern re-analyses do not confirm the arginine claim. No verifiable mechanism from HCN to arginine via hydrolysate is specified. Should be treated as historically interesting but not plausible.', 'Urea-cycle-mimicking pathway via citrulline: carbamoylation of ornithine is straightforward chemistry, but conversion to arginine (rxn_007) is neither atom-balanced nor demonstrated prebiotically. No experimental data supports the non-enzymatic citrulline-to-arginine step. Environmental progression is reasonable but steps have concatenation uncertainties.', 'Speculative C5 skeleton construction (malononitrile to keto acid) is not atom-balanced (rxn_010) nor literature-supported for the arginine skeleton. The hydrothermal context is possible for similar transformations, but no relevant studies found for these substrates.', 'Utilizes photolytic production of cyanamide and guanidine, which is literature-substantiated (Lohrmann 1972), but the downstream steps (rxn_003, rxn_006) are again unbalanced and unverified for arginine. Mechanistic plausibility drops steeply at guanidinylation and homologation stages.', 'This reduction/guanidinylation sequence is highly speculative, failing atom balance checks and with no direct literature precedent