mediate and lose water.", "reasoning": "By analogy to aminonitrile guanidinylation (Patel et al.), though with aldehyde reduction to amine first; mechanism and side-products given. Atom/loss balanced."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Guanidinopropionitrile homologation to arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["NH3"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Strecker-like addition"}, "mechanism": "Further nucleophilic addition of ammonia to convert nitrile to aminonitrile (precursor to arginine via hydrolysis).", "reasoning": "Supported by inference in Patel et al. (2015), Step 45\u219246, tabled at 90% yield. Atom-balanced (NH3 addition)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrolysis of arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile to arginine", "inputs": ["mol_016", "2H2O"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "330\u2013370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "acid (e.g., H+ from clay/mineral)", "aqueous": true, "other": "Extended hydrolysis, possible surface catalysis"}, "mechanism": "Acid- or mineral-catalyzed hydrolysis of the aminonitrile to arginine with release of ammonia.", "reasoning": "No experiment yet shown this for arginine aminonitrile specifically (Patel et al., 2015), but is well-established for Strecker products generally. Atom and charge balanced. Marked speculative for arginine."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Photolytic formation of cyanamide from HCN and ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["UV light", "phosphate mineral"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "phosphate", "aqueous": true, "other": "Strong UV flux"}, "mechanism": "UV-induced oxidation and recombination of cyanide and ammonia to form cyanamide (and/or guanidine or urea, depending on photon flux).", "reasoning": "Demonstrated by Lohrmann (1972), with yields of cyanamide, urea, and guanidine in aqueous NH4CN solutions under UV. Atom balance checked."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Alkaline hydrolysis and rearrangement of HCN polymer to ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["base (NH3)", "heat"], "conditions": {"temperature": "360\u2013370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "NH3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Prolonged heating, alkaline"}, "mechanism": "Polymerization of HCN with ammonia, followed by base-catalyzed hydrolysis/rearrangement to form ornithine and guanidine.", "reasoning": "Ornithine reported as minor product in hot NH4CN or HCN/NH4OH hydrolysates (Labadie et al. 1967/68; Ferris et al. 1973); atom balanced to reflect guanidine co-product. No arginine ever confirmed present. Included as a credible ornithine-generation route."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Ornithine guanidinylation by cyanamide", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "H2O"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "330\u2013350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": null}, "mechanism": "Nucleophilic addition of ornithine \u03b4-amine to cyanamide, forming the arginine side chain, with water elimination.", "reasoning": "Demonstrated on peptides and post-translationally by Longo et al. (2020, PNAS); direct prebiotic guanidinylation of monomeric ornithine not yet shown. Atom and charge balanced. Noted as a plausible but unconfirmed route for free arginine."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Carbamoylation of ornithine to citrulline", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": null}, "mechanism": "Addition of cyanate to ornithine \u03b4-am