m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Arginine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: This network presents ten mechanistically distinct and chemically justified prebiotic routes to arginine, structured as a bipartite graph from simple C1/N feedstocks (HCN, NH3, cyanamide, acetylene) and geochemically plausible intermediates, encompassing all currently evidenced cyanosulfidic, ornithine-guanidinylation, and biosynthetic-mimicking pathways, as well as surveyed speculative mechanisms. All atom and charge balances for molecule transformations have been tracked explicitly at the reaction level, with small-molecule byproducts (H2O, NH3, etc.) where necessary. Each pathway highlights its experimentally supported terminus per the latest literature: (1) cyanosulfidic network (Patel et al. 2015) proven to guanidinylated arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile only; (2) post-hoc guanidinylation of ornithine (Longo et al. 2020) supported only for peptides or monomeric ornithine; (3) HCN-polymer hydrolytic detection is flagged as disputed/no longer credible. Later stages (hydrolysis or non-enzymatic amidination) are correctly explicit as speculative and marked. Catalysts, minerals and environments are selected on geochemical merit for the specific chemistry and referenced in each reaction. Hub molecules (ornithine, guanidine, cyanamide, \u03b2-aminopropionitrile, guanidinylated intermediates) are annotated where their usage is convergent. All 10 pathways are uniquely traceable from feedstock to arginine, via molecular steps that reflect current prebiotic chemistry constraints and literature consensus.\n\nMOLECULES (16 nodes):\n  - mol_001: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: C2H2 (acetylene) [starting]\n  - mol_004: CH3CN (acetonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_005: C3H3N (acrylonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_006: C3H7N2 (\u03b2-aminopropionitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_007: CH2N2 (cyanamide) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C2H4N4 (guanidine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C3H7N3 (guanidinylated \u03b2-aminopropionitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C3H7N (propylamine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C5H10N2O2 (ornithine) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C5H12N4O2 (arginine) [target]\n  - mol_013: C5H11N3O (citrulline) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: OCN (cyanate ion) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: CON2H4 (urea) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C5H11N5 (arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (14 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_003 -> mol_005 (HCN\u2013acetylene cross-coupling to acrylonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_005, mol_002 -> mol_006 (Acrylonitrile conjugate addition of ammonia) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_006, mol_007 -> mol_009, mol_002 (\u03b2-aminopropionitrile guanidinylation with cyanamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_006 -> mol_010 (Photoreduction of \u03b2-aminopropionitrile to \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_010, mol_007 -> mol_009, H2O (\u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde guanidinylation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_009, mol_002 -> mol_016 (Guanidinopropionitrile homologation to arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_016, 2H2O -> mol_012, mol_002 (Hydrolysis of arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile to arginine) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_008: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_007 (Photol