mino_acids_and_evolution_of_the_triplet_code)\n  33. Order of amino acid recruitment into the genetic code resolved by last universal common ancestor\u2019s protein domains (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2410311121)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Arginine is treated as a difficult late target. The network therefore prioritizes the two best-supported precursor frameworks in the literature: a surface cyanosulfidic route that reaches the arginine alpha-aminonitrile from acrylonitrile and 3-aminopropanenitrile, and an ornithine-first route in which ornithine is generated by HCN chemistry or spark-discharge chemistry and then guanidinylated by cyanamide. Because free arginine itself has not been experimentally obtained under defended prebiotic conditions, the hydrolysis of the arginine aminonitrile and the free-ornithine guanidinylation step are included explicitly but flagged as speculative endgames.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrocyanic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-enenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-diaminopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ornithine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H13N5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-guanidinopentanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H14N4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-guanidinopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"arginine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"UV photolysis of ammonium cyanide to cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation of concentrated ammonium cyanide solution in anoxic surface pools\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photochemical conversion of ammonium cyanide solutions generates small C-N products including cyanamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Lohrmann showed that aqueous ammonium cyanide exposed to sunlight or unfiltered 254 nm UV yields cyanamide together with urea and guanidine (Lohrmann, 1972). This is the most direct literature-supported source of the guanidinylating reagent required for arginine-directed chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-mediated coupling of hydrogen cyanide and acetylene to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/phosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated near-neutral phosphate-buffered solution; localized cyanide- and sulfide-rich setting\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu(I)-mediated cyanocupration or cross-coupling of acetylene with HCN gives the activated C3 nitrile acrylonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The acrylonitrile entry point is directly reported in the cyanosulfidic network of Patel and co-workers (Patel et al., 2015). The required sulfidic-anion and UV context is plausible only in local surface settings rather than globally abundant environments, which is an important limitation (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia addition to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Near-neutral aqueous solution, compatible with cyanosulfidic surface flow chemistry\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds by conjugate addition to the activated alkene nitrile, yielding 3-aminopropanenitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. identify beta-aminopropionitrile 34 as the branch-point intermediate relevant to arginine chemistry and obtain it from ammonia addition to acrylonitrile (Patel et al., 2015). This is one of the cleanest experimentally defended arginine-relevant steps.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"H2S-driven photoreduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/phosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation with HS-/H2S as photoreductant in near-neutral aqueous solution\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoreductive conversion of the nitrile through sulfur-containing reduced intermediates yields the corresponding amino aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. report H2S-driven photoreduction of beta-aminopropionitrile 34 to beta-aminopropionaldehyde 36, with a one-step yield of 45% in the reported yield table (Patel et al., 2015). This makes the aldehyde branch experimentally grounded even though it still depends on contingent reagent sequencing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidinylation-first cyanosulfidic homologation to the arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/phosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated HS-/H2S-containing solution; sequential homologation and Strecker-like installation likely required rather than a single uncontrolled one-pot\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"3-Aminopropanenitrile is first guanidinylated by cyanamide and then extended through HCN-based homologation and terminal aminonitrile formation to give the arginine aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. demonstrated cyanamide guanidinylation of 34 to 35 and tabulated a high-yield later step from 45 to 46, the arginine alpha-aminonitrile precursor (Patel et al., 2015). This reaction node compresses that documented nitrile-first branch. It is literature-guided but still partly inferential because the integrated production of 46 from simple feedstocks was suggested rather than reported as free arginine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldehyde-adduct cyanosulfidic homologation to the arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/phosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated HS-/H2S-containing solution; cyanamide capture of the amino aldehyde followed by homologation and Strecker chemistry\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The amino aldehyde is efficiently trapped by cyanamide, then carried through further cyanosulfidic extension to the arginine alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. report efficient reaction of beta-aminopropionaldehyde 36 with cyanamide to give adduct 37, summarize 34 to 37 as 77% over two steps, and list 45 to 46 at 90% (Patel et al., 2015). This makes the aldehyde-adduct route the strongest experimentally supported arginine-directed precursor branch, though it still stops short of free arginine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Mild pool hydrolysis of the arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Prolonged residence in concentrated prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces; mildly acidic to neutral water\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Stepwise hydration of the nitrile to amide and carboxylic acid gives free arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. Patel et al. stop at the arginine alpha-aminonitrile and the supplied literature review found no published prebiotic experiment converting that aminonitrile to free arginine (Patel et al., 2015). The step is retained because aminonitrile hydrolysis is general chemistry, and a mild pool setting is preferred to avoid the strong-acid or high-heat conditions under which arginine itself can degrade.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hot HCN oligomer chemistry to ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated NH4CN or HCN/NH4OH solutions, hours to days, historical product assignments after hydrolytic workup\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN oligomerization followed by hydrolytic release yields trace diamino-acid products including ornithine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ruiz-Bermejo and co-workers summarize older reports of ornithine formation from hot ammonium cyanide or HCN/NH4OH chemistry (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). This route is included only as a low-confidence scaffold source because historical HCN-polymer amino-acid assignments are difficult to validate, and later GC-MS reanalyses did not confirm arginine claims (Ferris et al., 1973; Ferris et al., 1974).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Volcanic spark-discharge synthesis of ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Electrical discharge; condensate 280-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Reduced volcanic gas mixture with aqueous condensate enabling Strecker-type downstream chemistry\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Plasma chemistry generates nitriles, aldehydes, and reduced C-N fragments that condense into aqueous amino-acid mixtures including ornithine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Modern reanalysis of Miller archival spark-discharge samples detected ornithine in volcanic and sulfidic experiments, whereas arginine was not observed (Johnson et al., 2008; Parker et al., 2011). The route is therefore chemically useful only as an ornithine entry point.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Aqueous guanidinylation of ornithine by cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated pools, membrane-confined water, or mineral-interface microenvironments; neutral to mildly basic pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal delta-amine of ornithine reacts with cyanamide to install the guanidinium side chain, yielding arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Longo et al. showed that chemical guanidination converts ornithine side chains to arginine side chains in water and argued that cyanamide could have served as a primordial reagent (Longo et al., 2020). The supplied review notes that efficient, selective guanidinylation of free ornithine by cyanamide in water has not been published, so this monomer reaction is plausible but explicitly speculative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN polymer aging and fragmentation to cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated HCN/NH3 mixtures undergoing oligomerization, aging, and partial hydrolytic fragmentation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN polymer chemistry releases small C-N molecules, including cyanamide, as oligomers age and fragment.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ruiz-Bermejo et al. identify cyanamide among the small molecules generated by HCN polymer chemistry (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). Mapping this chemistry as a direct HCN plus NH3 to cyanamide step is simplified, but it is literature-based and provides a second prebiotic source of the same key guanidinylating reagent.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Subseafloor hydrothermal hydrolysis of the arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Short-residence-time subseafloor pore waters to limit secondary decomposition of arginine\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hot water promotes nitrile hydration to the corresponding amino acid, potentially yielding arginine transiently.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. No hydrothermal arginine-aminonitrile hydrolysis experiment was identified in the supplied literature. This alternative is included because nitriles are generally hydrolysable in hot water, but the review stresses that arginine itself is vulnerable under harsh hydrolytic conditions. Hydrothermal support is therefore only broad thermodynamic permissibility, not direct kinetics for this substrate (Amend and Shock, 1998).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"UV cyanamide plus nitrile-first cyanosulfidic route with mild pool hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Cyanamide from irradiated ammonium cyanide feeds the Patel nitrile-first branch to the arginine alpha-aminonitrile, followed by speculative mild hydrolysis in prebiotic pools.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"UV cyanamide plus nitrile-first cyanosulfidic route with hydrothermal hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The same nitrile-first cyanosulfidic precursor route is coupled to a hotter, more speculative subseafloor hydrolysis endgame.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"UV cyanamide plus aldehyde-adduct cyanosulfidic route with mild pool hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The best-supported aldehyde-capture branch passes through beta-aminopropionaldehyde before converging on the arginine alpha-aminonitrile and then undergoing speculative mild hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"UV cyanamide plus aldehyde-adduct cyanosulfidic route with hydrothermal hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The aldehyde-adduct precursor sequence is coupled to a subseafloor hydrothermal nitrile hydrolysis endgame.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Polymer-derived cyanamide plus nitrile-first cyanosulfidic route with mild pool hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN polymer aging supplies cyanamide, which is then used in the nitrile-first cyanosulfidic branch to the arginine alpha-aminonitrile, followed by mild hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Polymer-derived cyanamide plus nitrile-first cyanosulfidic route with hydrothermal hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"An HCN-polymer source of cyanamide is paired with the nitrile-first cyanosulfidic skeleton-building route and a hotter hydrothermal hydrolysis endgame.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Polymer-derived cyanamide plus aldehyde-adduct cyanosulfidic route with mild pool hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN polymer chemistry supplies cyanamide, while the aldehyde branch of the cyanosulfidic network furnishes the arginine alpha-aminonitrile before mild hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_0